t with the table above. A good check is that the watch\nLTHR (168) and the Intervals LTHR (172) are different.\n\n## Which parts of the colleague's note helped\n\nUsed:\n- The main point: `value` gives a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target that the watch\n  clamps, while `start` gives an Instant-HR corridor. Our symptoms match\n  theirs exactly, including the same LTHR values 172/168 and the same Float\n  75–82% → 129–141 example.\n- Keeping percentages (`%lthr`) instead of absolute bpm. It confirms our\n  earlier failed attempts (`bpm` → 214–249, `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` → constant\n  101) were dead ends, so I didn't try them again.\n- The verification method: push → readback → sync, and compare with\n  percent × Intervals LTHR while the two LTHR values differ.\n\nSkipped:\n- The \"Bonus trap\" about the top-level `description` and the text grammar\n  (`m` = minutes, `mtr`/`km`, `Z2` → power, and so on). It covers a different\n  symptom: steps not showing in the web UI. Nobody reported that, and the\n  readback shows steps are populated. Adding a `description` would bring a new\n  risk, since a parser error can wipe both representations, and it doesn't fix\n  the reported bug.\n- The search history (the UI dropdown, the \"absolute bpm is impossible\"\n  idea). It's background and adds nothing to the fix.\n- The parts about API credentials and creating or deleting a test event. They\n  need external calls, which aren't allowed here.\n"}}}
