{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgS85yRPUUgmTdrD1ozdH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the generator in `gen.py:10`: it now writes the lower bound of the heart-rate range as `start` instead of `value`. I checked only the generator output; nothing was pushed to Intervals or synced to the watch.\n\n**Why it was wrong:** with `{value, end}`, Intervals exports the range as a target tied to heart-rate zones, and the watch replaces it with its own nearest zone. That matches `readback/watch-notes.md`: every range the watch showed was exactly one of its zones (118–137, 137–151, 151–162). With `{start, end}`, Intervals exports the range in bpm, calculated from the threshold heart rate stored in Intervals (172), so the watch's own threshold (168) no longer affects it. Targets stay in percent of threshold heart rate (`%lthr`).\n\n**What I checked:** `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now writes `start`/`end` in all four heart-rate steps, and no `value` is left.\n\n**Still to do:** push the event, sync the watch, and compare it with the expected ranges: warm-up 117–129, tempo 151–160, float 129–141, cool-down 112–124.\n\nFrom the colleague's note I used three parts:\n- the `value` → `start` rename itself;\n- keeping percentages instead of absolute bpm;\n- the check method: compare with percent × the Intervals threshold while the watch's threshold is different.\n\nI skipped the part about the top-level `description` and its text format. It fixes a different problem (steps missing in the Intervals web UI), which wasn't reported here, and the readback shows the steps came through. All of this is written up in `ANSWER.md`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1338,"cache_read_input_tokens":24915,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1338},"output_tokens":546,"se